      <div class="paper-meta-bar">
        <span class="paper-tag">Evolutionary Genomics & Language Models</span>
        <span class="paper-venue-badge">bioRxiv 2025.09.21.677619</span>
        <span class="paper-time-badge"><Icon name="clock" size={14} /> {readingTime}</span>
      </div>

      <h1 class="paper-title">{title}</h1>

      <div class="paper-authors-box">
        <div><strong>Authors:</strong> Chengzhong Ye*, Gonzalo Benegas*, Carlos Albors, Jianan Canal Li, Sebastian Prillo, Peter D. Fields, Brian Clarke, and Yun S. Song*</div>
        <div><strong>Affiliation:</strong> University of California, Berkeley (Departments of Statistics, EECS, IGI); The Jackson Laboratory; German Cancer Research Center (DKFZ)</div>
        <div><strong>DOI:</strong> <a href="https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.09.21.677619" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="color: var(--color-accent);">10.1101/2025.09.21.677619</a> · Corresponding Author: Yun S. Song (<code>yss@berkeley.edu</code>)</div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- Lead / Overview -->
    <p class="paper-lead">
      Why do massive, multi-billion parameter genomic language models (gLMs) still struggle to outperform classical evolutionary conservation scores on variant effect prediction? Researchers from <strong>UC Berkeley (Song Lab)</strong> introduce <strong>GPN-Star</strong> (<strong>G</strong>enomic <strong>P</strong>retrained <strong>N</strong>etwork with <strong>S</strong>pecies <strong>T</strong>ree and <strong>A</strong>lignment <strong>R</strong>epresentation), a biologically grounded transformer architecture that explicitly integrates <strong>Whole-Genome Multi-Species Alignments (WGA)</strong> and <strong>phylogenetic species trees</strong> to set new state-of-the-art benchmarks across human genetics and model organisms.
    </p>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="paper-toc">
      <div class="paper-toc-title">
        <Icon name="list" size={14} /> Table of Contents
      </div>
      <ol class="paper-toc-list">
        <li><a href="#summary">1. Executive Summary & Paradigm Shift</a></li>
        <li><a href="#limits-glms">2. The Limits of Alignment-Free gLMs</a></li>
        <li><a href="#architecture">3. The Phylogeny-Aware Transformer Core</a></li>
        <li><a href="#fire-encoding">4. Clade Pooling & Evolutionary Distance via FIRE</a></li>
        <li><a href="#timescales">5. Three Evolutionary Timescales (Vertebrate, Mammal, Primate)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#results">6. Section-by-Section Results Breakdown (Figures 1–6)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#deeprvat">7. Rare Variant Association Testing in UK Biobank (DeepRVAT)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#formulations">8. Mathematical Formulations & Training Loss</a></li>
        <li><a href="#conclusions">9. Discussion, Model Organisms & Future Horizons</a></li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <!-- Chapter 1 -->
    <section id="summary">
      <h2>1. Executive Summary & The Core Paradigm Shift</h2>
      <p>
        Natural selection preserves functional genomic sequences across evolutionary timescales. While classical site-independent phylogenetic tools (such as <strong>phyloP</strong> and <strong>phastCons</strong>) effectively measure cross-species conservation, they cannot capture context-dependent epistasis or higher-order sequence syntax.
      </p>
      <p>
        Conversely, modern alignment-free genomic language models (e.g. <strong>HyenaDNA</strong>, <strong>Nucleotide Transformer</strong>, <strong>Evo</strong>, <strong>Evo-2</strong>) train on raw, unaligned DNA sequences across species. Despite scaling to billions of parameters, they lack explicit knowledge of phylogenetic branching and evolutionary distances, resulting in suboptimal single-nucleotide variant pathogenicity scoring.
      </p>

      <div class="paper-callout">
        <div class="paper-callout-title">
          <Icon name="sparkles" size={15} /> The GPN-Star Architectural Breakthrough
        </div>
        <p>
          <strong>GPN-Star</strong> bridges the gap between deep language models and phylogenetic evolution. Operating directly on Whole-Genome Alignments, GPN-Star incorporates:
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>Clade Attention Pooling:</strong> Dynamically condenses closely related species to eliminate phylogenetic redundancy without information loss.
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>Phylogeny-Informed Cross-Attention:</strong> Injects pairwise evolutionary distance biases derived from species tree branch lengths via Functional Interpolation for Relative Positional Encoding (FIRE).
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>Sequence-wise Self-Attention with RoPE:</strong> Anchors spatial coordinate queries on the primary target human genome (hg38).
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="paper-table-wrap">
        <table class="paper-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Evaluation Metric / Dimension</th>
              <th>Classical Scores (phyloP / phastCons)</th>
              <th>Alignment-Free gLMs (Evo / HyenaDNA / NT)</th>
              <th>Phylogeny-Aware GPN-Star (Song Lab)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Spatial Context Modeling</strong></td>
              <td>Site-independent (treats each nucleotide in isolation).</td>
              <td>Models broad sequence context, but blind to tree topology.</td>
              <td><strong>Integrates spatial genomic context with explicit species tree branch lengths.</strong></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Phylogenetic Branching</strong></td>
              <td>Tree-based neutral rate scaling.</td>
              <td>Ignored (treats all training species as an unstructured bag of sequences).</td>
              <td><strong>Continuous non-linear species tree distance encoding via FIRE.</strong></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Zero-Shot Pathogenicity</strong></td>
              <td>Moderate across coding; weak on regulatory enhancers.</td>
              <td>Inferior to phyloP despite billions of parameters.</td>
              <td><strong>State-of-the-Art across ClinVar, COSMIC, OMIM, HGMD, and UK Biobank.</strong></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Complex Trait Heritability (S-LDSC)</strong></td>
              <td>Moderate enrichment.</td>
              <td>Low conditional informativeness (<Katex tex="\tau^*" />).</td>
              <td><strong>Outperforms Enformer and Borzoi on tissue-agnostic trait heritability.</strong></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Rare Variant Association (DeepRVAT)</strong></td>
              <td>Baseline discovery yield.</td>
              <td>N/A</td>
              <td><strong>Boosts WES gene discovery from 383 to 402 significant genes in UK Biobank.</strong></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 2 -->
    <section id="limits-glms">
      <h2>2. The Limits of Alignment-Free Genomic Language Models</h2>
      <p>
        Billion-parameter genomic foundation models treat genomes analogously to natural language corpora—training masked or autoregressive language models across unaligned multi-species reference genomes. However, this paradigm faces three fundamental biological failure modes:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>The Phylogenetic Skew Dilemma:</strong> Public genome databases are heavily biased toward sequenced model clades (e.g. dozens of closely related rodents or primates). Without phylogenetic weighting, language models overfit to clade-specific neutral substitutions rather than true purifying selection.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Inability to Distinguish Divergence from Constraint:</strong> An unaligned language model cannot distinguish whether a nucleotide difference between human and mouse represents a neutral lineage-specific divergence or a deleterious mutation disrupting a conserved essential function.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Lack of Orthologous Base Correspondence:</strong> Without whole-genome alignment coordinates, models must learn positional orthology from scratch across millions of base pairs, wasting model capacity on alignment reconstruction rather than biochemical grammar.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 3 -->
    <section id="architecture">
      <h2>3. The Phylogeny-Aware Transformer Core</h2>
      <p>
        GPN-Star processes multi-species alignment blocks <Katex tex="\mathbf{X} \in \mathbb{R}^{N_S \times L}" />, where <Katex tex="N_S" /> is the number of species (e.g. 241 mammals in Zoonomia or 44 primates) and <Katex tex="L" /> is the sequence context window (<Katex tex="128\text{ bp}" /> to <Katex tex="256\text{ bp}" />).
      </p>

      <div class="paper-callout callout-biology">
        <div class="paper-callout-title">
          <Icon name="cpu" size={15} /> Layer-by-Layer Architecture Pipeline
        </div>
        <p>
          &bull; <strong>1. Target & Non-Target Decomposition:</strong> The target genome sequence (e.g. human hg38) is embedded into <Katex tex="E^{(T)} \in \mathbb{R}^{N_T \times L \times D}" />, while non-target species sequences are embedded into <Katex tex="E^{(S)} \in \mathbb{R}^{N_S \times L \times D}" />.
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>2. Clade Attention Pooling:</strong> Non-target species within each predefined phylogenetic clade are condensed along the species axis into clade representative embeddings <Katex tex="E^{(C)} \in \mathbb{R}^{N_C \times L \times D}" />.
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>3. Sequence-wise Self-Attention with RoPE:</strong> Standard transformer attention along the spatial sequence axis <Katex tex="L" /> using Rotational Position Embeddings (RoPE), updating target token representations.
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>4. Phylogeny-Informed Cross-Attention with FIRE:</strong> Target representations attend across all clade representations at each spatial column <Katex tex="j" />, with attention logits modulated by species tree branch length distance biases <Katex tex="b(\Phi)" />.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 4 -->
    <section id="fire-encoding">
      <h2>4. Clade Pooling & Evolutionary Distance Encoding via FIRE</h2>

      <h3>Clade Attention Pooling</h3>
      <p>
        To prevent closely related species from dominating computation and skewing representations, GPN-Star partitions non-target species into <Katex tex="N_C" /> clades based on a phylogenetic distance threshold <Katex tex="\phi_{\text{clade}}" />. For each clade <Katex tex="c" />, member species at alignment column <Katex tex="j" /> are condensed into a single representative embedding:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Clade Attention Pooling Formulation</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\text{Attn}_{\text{pool}}(x^{(s)}_j, s \in c) = \sum_{s \in c} \text{softmax}\left( x^{(s)}_j W_{\alpha} + b(\phi_{c,s}) \right) x^{(s)}_j W_v" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="x^{(s)}_j" /> = Embedding of species <Katex tex="s" /> at column <Katex tex="j" /> &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="\phi_{c, s}" /> = Evolutionary distance from species <Katex tex="s" /> to the clade root &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="b(\cdot)" /> = Learnable continuous distance bias.
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Phylogeny-Informed Cross-Attention with FIRE</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Functional Interpolation for Relative Positional Encoding (FIRE)</strong> maps continuous phylogenetic distances <Katex tex="\phi" /> (substitutions per neutral site) into attention biases through a two-layer Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) with SiLU activation:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Phylogeny-Informed Cross-Attention & FIRE Bias</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\text{Attn}_{\text{phy}}(E^{(T)}_j, E^{(C)}_j) = \text{softmax}\left( \frac{(E^{(T)}_j W_Q^{\text{phy}})(E^{(C)}_j W_K^{\text{phy}})^T}{\sqrt{D}} + b(\Phi) \right) E^{(C)}_j W_V^{\text{phy}}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body" style="margin-top: var(--space-3);">
          <Katex tex="b(\phi) = f_{\theta}\left( \frac{\log(c\phi + 1)}{\log(c\phi_{\max} + 1)} \right), \quad f_{\theta}(x) = v_2^T \text{SiLU}(v_1 x)" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="\Phi_{s_1, s_2}" /> = Evolutionary tree distance between species <Katex tex="s_1" /> and <Katex tex="s_2" /> &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="c, \phi_{\max}" /> = Scaling constants &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="v_1, v_2" /> = Learnable MLP projection vectors.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 5 -->
    <section id="timescales">
      <h2>5. Three Evolutionary Timescales (Vertebrate, Mammal, Primate)</h2>
      <p>
        The authors train three distinct GPN-Star models spanning different evolutionary depths:
      </p>

      <div class="paper-table-wrap">
        <table class="paper-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Evolutionary Timescale</th>
              <th>Alignment Scope</th>
              <th>Time Depth</th>
              <th>Optimal Biological Niche</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Vertebrate GPN-Star (V)</strong></td>
              <td>100-way Vertebrate WGA</td>
              <td>~450 Million Years</td>
              <td>Deep macro-evolutionary conservation: core coding exons, universal promoters, and essential molecular machinery.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Mammal GPN-Star (M)</strong></td>
              <td>241-way Zoonomia WGA</td>
              <td>~100 Million Years</td>
              <td>Balanced conservation: mammalian developmental enhancers, alternative splice junctions, and protein-protein interfaces.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Primate GPN-Star (P)</strong></td>
              <td>44-way Primate WGA</td>
              <td>~65 Million Years</td>
              <td>Recent lineage-specific human regulatory syntax: candidate cis-regulatory elements (cCREs), transcription factor binding sites, and complex trait heritability.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="paper-callout">
        <div class="paper-callout-title">
          <Icon name="sparkles" size={15} /> Why Primate Models Dominate Human Genetics
        </div>
        <p>
          Deep vertebrate alignments are too diverged to align non-coding human promoters and enhancers reliably. The <strong>Primate GPN-Star model</strong> achieves the highest accuracy on human non-coding variant pathogenicity and complex trait heritability because primate genomes retain intact orthologous cis-regulatory architecture.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

