    <!-- Chapter 7 -->
    <section id="deeprvat">
      <h2>7. Rare Variant Association Testing in UK Biobank (DeepRVAT)</h2>
      <p>
        In rare variant association testing (RVAT), single-marker tests lack statistical power. <strong>DeepRVAT</strong> aggregates rare variants within each gene using predictive deleteriousness weights.
      </p>

      <div class="paper-callout callout-biology">
        <div class="paper-callout-title">
          <Icon name="check-circle" size={15} /> Whole-Exome Sequencing (WES) Discovery in 161,822 UK Biobank Individuals
        </div>
        <p>
          Integrating GPN-Star variant effect predictions into DeepRVAT across 34 quantitative traits:
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>Increased Gene Discovery:</strong> Boosted gene-level discoveries from <strong>383 to 402 significant genes</strong> (<Katex tex="\text{FWER} < 0.05" />).
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>High Replication Rate:</strong> 353 of the 402 genes replicated in independent larger cohorts (compared to 338 for baseline annotations).
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 8 -->
    <section id="formulations">
      <h2>8. Mathematical Formulations & Training Loss</h2>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Repeat-Masked & Conservation-Weighted MLM Loss</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\mathcal{L}_{\text{MLM}} = - \frac{1}{|\mathcal{M}|} \sum_{(t, j) \in \mathcal{M}} w_j \log \hat{p}\left( \acute{x}^{(t)}_j \mid \{\tilde{x}^{(t)}\}_{t \in T}, \{\tilde{x}^{(s)}\}_{s \in S} \right)" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body" style="margin-top: var(--space-3);">
          <Katex tex="w_j \propto (0.1 \cdot \mathbb{I}_{\text{repeat}} + \mathbb{I}_{\neg\text{repeat}}) \cdot \max(\text{phyloP}_j, 1) \cdot (\text{phastCons}_{M, j} + 0.1)" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          Downweights repetitive transposable elements while upweighting conserved functional sites during masked token training.
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Zero-Shot Variant Scoring Statistics</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\text{LLR}(v) = \log\frac{p(\text{alt})}{p(\text{ref})}, \qquad H(p) = - \sum_{x \in \{A,C,G,T\}} p(x) \log p(x)" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="\text{LLR}(v)" /> measures directional allelic deleteriousness &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="H(p)" /> measures site constraint / mutational tolerance.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 9 -->
    <section id="conclusions">
      <h2>9. Discussion, Model Organisms & Future Horizons</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>GPN-Star</strong> demonstrates that the future of genomic language models lies in explicit evolutionary grounding. By replacing unaligned sequence bags with Whole-Genome Alignments and species tree branch lengths, GPN-Star sets a new gold standard for variant pathogenicity, heritability partitioning, and rare-variant discovery across eukaryotes.
      </p>

      <div class="paper-callout callout-biology">
        <div class="paper-callout-title">
          <Icon name="activity" size={15} /> Key Takeaways for the Community
        </div>
        <p>
          &bull; <strong>Phylogeny Trumps Parameter Scale:</strong> A biologically grounded 100M-parameter phylogeny-aware model outperforms 7B+ unaligned gLMs.
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>Timescale Matching:</strong> Primate models for human regulatory variants; Mammalian/Vertebrate models for universal coding machinery.
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>Universal Cross-Species Utility:</strong> Immediate out-of-the-box applicability to crops (*A. thaliana*), livestock (*G. gallus*), and model organisms (*D. melanogaster*, *C. elegans*).
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
